<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sukiyaki (Kenny Ball and his Jazzmen)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1963</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instrumental cover; gave the song its English title</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">My First Lonely Night (Jewel Akens)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1966</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, new English words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sukiyaki (A Taste of Honey)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1980</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new English words by Janice-Marie Johnson; US number three, 1981</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sukiyaki (Selena)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1989</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sukiyaki (4 P.M.)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1994</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover with A Taste of Honey's English words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sukiyaki Dohyo Chanko (Soichi Terada)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1996</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sampled the 4 P.M. cover</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Japan Post commemorative stamp</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1999</span><span class="sg-chain-h">stamp honouring the song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Suntory commercials after the Tōhoku earthquake</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2011</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung by 71 actors and singers</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">From Up on Poppy Hill (Studio Ghibli)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2011</span><span class="sg-chain-h">heard in the film</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sukiyaki (G. H. Hat, with Alina Renae and Eriko Tamura)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2018</span><span class="sg-chain-h">dance versions in English and Japanese</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Closing ceremony of the Tokyo Olympic Games</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2021</span><span class="sg-chain-h">remix performed</span></li></ul></section>
